WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.041
In this film you see how FundsXML records what moved in a fund: money from investors, payments to investors, income from the portfolio, and the fund's own trades.

2
00:00:11.091 --> 00:00:12.871
It takes about four minutes.

3
00:00:14.121 --> 00:00:16.538
Transactions do not live in one place.

4
00:00:16.588 --> 00:00:18.615
Each kind sits where it belongs.

5
00:00:18.665 --> 00:00:23.160
Subscriptions and redemptions of investors are flows of a share class.

6
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.925
Distributions to investors also belong to the share class.

7
00:00:26.975 --> 00:00:31.534
Income from the portfolio, such as dividends, belongs to the portfolio.

8
00:00:31.584 --> 00:00:35.819
And so do the fund's own trades, the buys and sells of securities.

9
00:00:36.777 --> 00:00:41.306
Here are the flows of the dollar share class of a fictional global equity fund.

10
00:00:41.356 --> 00:00:44.957
The container names its reporting period, the month of October.

11
00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:56.085
The first flow is a subscription: transaction type S U B, with trade date, accounting date and settlement date, one thousand ninety-five units, and the total value in the share class currency.

12
00:00:56.135 --> 00:01:01.938
Optional fields can add the value in the fund currency, the sales channel and the investor's country.

13
00:01:02.588 --> 00:01:09.504
The third flow is a redemption, transaction type R E D: two thousand one hundred eighty-nine units, paid out of the fund.

14
00:01:10.654 --> 00:01:11.358
And switches?

15
00:01:11.408 --> 00:01:19.245
When an investor moves from one share class to another, the flows show a redemption in the first class and a subscription in the second.

16
00:01:19.295 --> 00:01:21.855
The schema has no element that links the two.

17
00:01:21.905 --> 00:01:28.118
If your partner needs the link, agree on how to mark it, for example in the free-text subtype of both flows.

18
00:01:29.368 --> 00:01:32.515
The institutional euro class distributes its income.

19
00:01:32.565 --> 00:01:34.298
Its distribution is official.

20
00:01:34.348 --> 00:01:40.385
Then come its dates: announcement, record date, ex-date and payment date, and the payment currency.

21
00:01:40.435 --> 00:01:46.472
And the amounts per share: eighteen euro forty gross, and thirteen euro thirty-four net, after tax.

22
00:01:47.123 --> 00:01:51.490
Income from the portfolio is recorded in Earnings, again with a period.

23
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:56.279
This one is a dividend from Valemont Materials, a fictional American company.

24
00:01:56.329 --> 00:02:00.447
The asset unique ID links it to the security, just like a position.

25
00:02:00.497 --> 00:02:05.734
The entry value is given in dollars and in the fund currency, with the exchange rate.

26
00:02:05.784 --> 00:02:13.385
And the dividend block holds the details in dollars: the amount per share, gross, net, and fifteen percent withholding tax.

27
00:02:14.036 --> 00:02:18.250
Finally, the fund's own trades, in Transactions, for the same period.

28
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:26.714
Here is a buy: two thousand three hundred eleven shares of a fictional American company, with trade date, value date and number of units.

29
00:02:26.764 --> 00:02:33.820
Then the price, the market value in dollars and in euro, the settlement amount, the exchange rate and the expenses.

30
00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:39.813
And the broker as counterparty, with its LEI, and the trading venue, the New York Stock Exchange.

31
00:02:39.863 --> 00:02:43.149
Further up, a sale of a French share, settled in euro.

32
00:02:43.199 --> 00:02:46.856
Every trade points to its asset through the asset unique ID.

33
00:02:48.006 --> 00:02:50.174
A few tips for reading transactions.

34
00:02:50.224 --> 00:02:58.433
Use the period of each container: from and to say which days the data covers, so you know whether a day without flows really had none.

35
00:02:58.483 --> 00:03:03.795
Read the action code: C creates an entry, and later deliveries can modify or delete it.

36
00:03:03.845 --> 00:03:08.972
Expect two currencies: the share class or quotation currency, and the fund currency.

37
00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:14.396
And follow the links: earnings and trades point to their assets, exactly like positions.

38
00:03:15.746 --> 00:03:18.055
What does the core schema not cover?

39
00:03:18.105 --> 00:03:26.968
Orders and their execution, cut-off times and settlement instructions belong to the operational messages of standards such as ISO 20022.

40
00:03:27.018 --> 00:03:32.736
FundsXML reports what has happened, for a period: it is the record, not the instruction.

41
00:03:34.086 --> 00:03:36.671
That was transactions and investor flows.

42
00:03:36.721 --> 00:03:41.686
In the next film, you look at the regulatory templates inside a FundsXML file.

43
00:03:41.736 --> 00:03:42.972
Thanks for watching.
